DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/sound-voice-strings-compositi
 ons-for-traditional-east-asian-instruments\n\nAn evening of new music for 
 stringed traditional East Asian instruments. Featuring Katherine Whatley (
 koto and voice)\, Southeast of Rain (pipa\, voice\, electronics)\, and Ste
 lla Da Yun Song (haegeum).\n\nAbout the performers and pieces:\n\nKatherin
 e Whatley\n\nKatherine Whatley is a koto musician\, composer\, and PhD Can
 didate in East Asian Languages and Cultures at Stanford University. She pl
 ays both traditional and experimental koto\, both improvised and composed.
  Her compositions have been performed by the Stanford New Ensemble and the
  Earplay Ensemble. She has performed in a variety of contexts including at
  the Good Shepherd Center (Seattle)\, Columbia University (New York)\, on 
 tour across Japan and in Berlin.\n\nHer dissertation analyzes music and so
 undscape in medieval Japanese literature. Her research has previously been
  published in The February Journal\, positions\, Japanese Journal of Relig
 ious Studies\, and Migrating Minds. As a journalist\, she has worked with 
 the BBC\, Dwell\, Conde Nast\, New York Times\, the Japan Times\, and othe
 rs.\n\n今、箏ひびき(Ima Koto Hibiki)—Now\, Here Koto Sings\n\nThis
  piece for 13-string koto and voice is based on Ryōjin Hisho\, a 12th-cen
 tury collection of imayō (popular songs). Ryōjin Hisho draws from songs 
 taught to the emperor by Otomae\, a courtesan. Because no original scores 
 remain\, this composition starts from this point of unknown to have a conv
 ersation across time. The piece is designed to encourage improvisation\, w
